  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through Thursday)\nIssued at 1234 PM CST FRI NOV 13 2026\n\nBreezy northwest winds are providing a November chill today.\nWinds decrease tonight but may stay up a bit at unsheltered\nlocations. At sheltered locations, good radiational cooling will\nbe present, allowing for widespread frost and a light freeze. A\nFreeze Warning is in effect for all of Central Alabama tonight.\nSaturday will be another cool day with northerly winds\ncontinuing. Winds may be even lighter Saturday night as high\npressure strengthens, with slightly cooler low temperatures. For\nsimplicity, an additional Freeze Warning is in effect areawide\nSaturday night, and will wait until Sunday to determine which\ncounties have had their growing season (frost/freeze program)\nended.\n\nThe eastern CONUS trough exits by Sunday, while a strong ridge\nbuilds over the central CONUS. A southern stream upper low will\nundercut the ridge, before opening up into a wave by the time it\nreaches Alabama on Monday. Guidance continues to trend towards a\nmore suppressed solution regarding a weak wave of low pressure\ndeveloping at low-levels. Mid and upper-level lift/moisture will\nbe present over Central Alabama, but east winds will provide a\nsteady supply of dry air at low-levels. This will serve to\nevaporate much of any precipitation, but still wouldn't be\nsurprised if some sprinkles or very light rain made it down to\nthe ground, similar to this past Wednesday. Chances of measurable\nprecipitation remain in the 10-15% range in our far southern\ncounties. Mid-level ridging and surface high pressure will remain\nin place for the rest of next week. Dry conditions are expected\nwith warming high temperatures and cool mornings.\n\n32/JDavis",
